tating the rules for transmitting and receiving data in a wireless environment [Data: Entities (986); Relationships (1473)]. This ensures orderly communication and prevents data collisions.\\n\\nWi-Fi technology specifically leverages the MAC sublayer to control access to the wireless medium. This control is essential for managing multiple devices that may attempt to communicate simultaneously over the same wireless channel. The MAC sublayer's functions are fundamental to the efficient and reliable operation of Wi-Fi networks [Data: Entities (413); Relationships (1473)]. Furthermore, technologies like OFDMA (Orthogonal Frequency Division Multiple Access) directly influence how the MAC sublayer manages this access, optimizing the use of the wireless medium [Data: Relationships (1488)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Frequency Bands Utilized by Wi-Fi\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Wi-Fi operates across specific frequency bands, notably including the 5 GHz and 6 GHz bands. These frequency bands are essential for wireless local area networks, determining the channels through which Wi-Fi signals are transmitted and received [Data: Entities (989, 990); Relationships (1477, 1478)]. The choice of frequency band can impact factors such as range, speed, and interference levels.\\n\\nThe 5 GHz band, for example, offers higher bandwidth and less interference compared to the older 2.4 GHz band, leading to faster data transfer rates. The introduction of the 6 GHz band, particularly with newer Wi-Fi standards like Wi-Fi 6E and Wi-Fi 7, further expands the available spectrum, providing even greater capacity and reducing congestion. These advancements in frequency utilization are key to meeting the increasing demand for wireless connectivity [Data: Entities (413); Relationships (1477, 1478)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Evolution and Security of Wi-Fi Technology\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Wi-Fi technology has seen significan